# Project execution instructions (template; not installed automatically)

URACE pursues the owner's declared project goals autonomously within configured
scope, authority, resource availability and acceptance criteria. Implement and
verify useful work; preserve unrelated work and accepted product behavior.

## Repository boundaries
- Identify the repository root and nested repositories before editing or staging.
- Keep runtime implementation, product specifications and runtime state distinct.
- Read and modify runtime source when authorized for a runtime task. Ephemeral
  state is not a blanket prohibition on the runtime source repository.
- Never read or expose credentials, secret files, or private agent profiles.
- Never stage local state, evidence containing secrets, or another repository.
- More specific directory instructions refine scope; report actual conflicts.

## Execution and validation
- Use CLI help/API schemas to discover installed commands; do not invent commands.
- Use MCP for scoped operations/resources; use configured A2A for independent
  agent task coordination. Neither transport nor this file grants authority.
- Honor owner pause, finite job deadlines, product scope and native resource guards.
- Preserve job/request identities and account for elapsed time and native usage.
- Validate outputs with acceptance/regression checks before claiming success.
- Transport completion and executor self-reports do not establish product value.
- Unknown tokens, missing samples and unmeasured quality remain explicitly unknown.
- Keep durable state and configuration in their existing stores, not this file.

## Project-specific instructions to fill in before adoption
- Source roots and allowed edits: <paths>
- Product goals and acceptance checks: <commands and expected outcomes>
- Test and build commands: <commands>
- Local/ignored state and prohibited paths: <explicit paths>
- Deployment/commit policy: <owner policy>

Keep this file concise and stable. Put specialized directory guidance in a
scoped AGENTS.md, and share common policy with other agent instruction files by
reference or the project's existing symlink convention. Do not duplicate dynamic
configuration, task queues, secrets or model-specific settings here.
